            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <AtypicalElectricalLoads deIcingCables="false" exteriorLighting="false" hotTub="false" heatedGarage="true" portableAirConditioning="false" pool="false" mixedUse="false" totalUnits="0" energyStarUnits="0" />
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="3" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="3" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Your house is pretty good overall for the amount of air leakage, however there are a few nuisance locations

that should be addressed. Air leakage was observed through weather stripping, particularly around the back door off the dining room. Air leakage was also observed through the two older bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace these exhaust fans with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model. The ensuite bathroom fan appearedto have a loose connection between the fan and exhaust duct, this should be investigated. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 245&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



















-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2x2 strapping with R5 insulation. I have modelled adding 2 inches (R10) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R10 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 270&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;













-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

Overall, your windows around the home appear to be in good condition. For furture consideration, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 200&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 





























-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Combi Loop from Current Oil Boiler&lt;/b&gt;



You currently have a deticated loop connect to your 182L electric water heater from your boiler, however identified that it is not working correctly. Ideally, the deticated loop from the boiler would be able to maintain your hot water demand, without operating the electrical elements of the tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. I have modelled the potential savings of using this deticated loop year round, if you were to have this loop fixed/corrected. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your boiler to heat your hot water, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 800&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 

&lt;b&gt;Please note:&lt;/b&gt; The above savings are based on a national standard for hot water use. 





&lt;b&gt;Instantaneous Propane, On-Demand Hot Water&lt;/b&gt;



I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,100&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. &lt;b&gt;Please note:&lt;/b&gt; The above savings are based on a national standard for hot water use. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;b&gt;87% Oil-Fired Boiler&lt;/b&gt;



Your current oil fired boiler was originally rated at 80% AFUE, however a recent combustion test would indicate the current efficiency. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® oil boiler with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified oil furnace that has a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 600&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;20.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 







&lt;b&gt;High-Efficiency Propane Boiler&lt;/b&gt;



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. This type of boiler is direct vented out a side wall. With a propane fuel supply, you could also replace your current oil hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous on-demand propane hot water heater with a minimum .92 Energy Factor (EF). 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,300&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;36.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Pellet Stove&lt;/b&gt;



If you are considering installing a pellet stove, I recommend you install a pellet stove that is EPA Certified. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes, and includes a dedicated combustion air supply. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By installing an EPA certified pellet stove and using it for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 700&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

